course: everyday
lesson: 252
title: Heat, sports gear and mosquitoes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When you play hard on a hot day, your body loses water and salt in sweat; if you get dizzy, sick, very tired or get a headache, that can be heat exhaustion, and you stop, rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult.
explain: Heat exhaustion that is ignored can turn into heat stroke, which is an emergency: the person becomes confused or faints, and an adult calls the emergency number.
explain: Good gear keeps you safe only if it is right and in good condition: shoes that fit and suit the sport, and helmets, pads and straps without cracks or broken parts.
explain: Shared helmets, gloves and hats can pass on germs and head lice, so they are cleaned between users.
explain: A girl in Florida playing outside at dusk puts on long sleeves and insect repellent, because that is when mosquitoes bite most, and in some places they carry diseases.
fact: Heat exhaustion can make you dizzy, sick, very tired and give you a headache.
fact: With signs of heat exhaustion you rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult.
fact: Heat exhaustion can lead to heat stroke, which is an emergency.
fact: Drinking water before and during sport on hot days helps prevent heat exhaustion.
fact: Sports shoes should fit and suit the sport, because wrong shoes cause slips and injuries.
fact: Shared helmets and gloves are cleaned between users, because shared gear can spread germs and head lice.
fact: Before using equipment, you look for cracks, loose parts and broken straps.
fact: Broken gear causes injuries, so it is not used until it is mended or replaced.
fact: Many mosquitoes bite most at dusk (when it gets dark in the evening) and dawn (when it gets light in the morning).
fact: Repellent or long sleeves protect you from mosquito bites.
fact: In some places mosquitoes carry diseases.
exercise: During a football match on a hot day you feel dizzy and sick. What do you do? | Rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Keep playing until the final whistle, then go and have a rest | Run faster so that the game finishes sooner | Sit in the sun to warm up
exercise: What can heat exhaustion lead to if it is ignored? | Heat stroke | A cold | Frostbite | A sprained ankle
exercise: Which of these can be a sign of heat exhaustion? | Dizziness and a headache | A runny nose | A rash on your feet | Cold, blue fingers
exercise: How can you help prevent heat exhaustion at a summer sports day? | Drink water before and during the games | Drink nothing at all until the very end of the day | Wear a thick black jumper to keep the sun off | Play only in full sun
exercise: You are going to play soccer on grass. Which shoes are best? | Well-fitting football boots or trainers | Flip-flops, so your feet stay cool | Your school shoes with smooth soles | Your big brother's boots, even though they are too big
exercise: Why do shoes need to fit and suit the sport? | Wrong shoes cause slips and injuries | Coaches like all the team's shoes to be the same colour | Shoes that fit wear out faster | Big shoes make you run faster, because your steps are longer
exercise: Your team shares batting helmets. What should be done? | Clean them between users | Nothing at all | Wear two at once | Share only with friends
exercise: What can spread through shared helmets and hats? | Germs and head lice | Sunburn from the last person who wore it | Broken bones from old falls | Asthma and hay fever
exercise: You are about to put on your bike helmet. What do you check? | Cracks, loose parts and broken straps | Only that you like its colour | Whether it smells nice inside | Nothing, because helmets last forever
exercise: Your skateboard has a loose wheel. What do you do? | Do not ride it until it is fixed | Ride it slowly, but only downhill | Ride it on the road, where it is smoother | Lend it to a friend until it is fixed
exercise: You are playing outside at dusk in summer. How do you protect yourself from mosquitoes? | Wear repellent or long sleeves | Stand very still so they cannot find you | Wear shorts and a vest to stay cool | Eat lots of sweets, because mosquitoes hate sugar
exercise: When do many mosquitoes bite most? | At dusk and dawn | At midday | Only in winter | Only indoors
exercise: Why does it matter to avoid mosquito bites in some places? | Mosquitoes can carry diseases there | Bites there make your hair fall out | Mosquitoes there steal food from your plate | Bites turn your skin blue
exercise: Your friend's knee pads have a broken strap. Why should he not use them? | Broken gear can cause injuries | The pads will look untidy in the team photo | The coach dislikes the colour of them | They will be much too warm with a broken strap
test: A boy in Arizona plays basketball outside at noon in July. He stops sweating, feels confused and almost faints. What is right? | It may be heat stroke: an adult calls the emergency number | He should keep playing to cool down | He just needs a sweet snack | It is a normal tired feeling
test: Two girls swap riding helmets at a stable without cleaning them. What could pass between them? | Head lice and germs | Their riding skills | Their hair colour | Their height
test: A boy in Queensland is at a barbecue at sunset by a lake. Which is the best choice? | Put on repellent and long sleeves | Take his shirt off | Stand near the water to cool off | Do nothing, mosquitoes only bite at noon
test: A girl finds a crack in her ski helmet. What should she do? | Not use it and get it replaced | Cover the crack with tape and ski | Use it only on easy slopes | Paint over the crack
test: A boy plays tennis in his big brother's trainers, which are two sizes too big. What can happen? | He may slip, trip or twist his ankle | He will run faster with longer steps | Nothing at all | His feet will soon grow to fit them
test: On a hot sports day, a girl feels very tired and has a headache. Which is the best first step? | Move to the shade, drink water and tell a teacher | Run one more race to shake off the tiredness and headache | Sit in the hot sun for a while to rest and dry off | Drink nothing and wait quietly for it to pass all by itself
test: Why do some places with mosquitoes take bites more seriously than others? | In some places mosquitoes carry diseases | Mosquitoes there are much bigger | They bite only children there | The bites there are far more itchy than anywhere else
test: A team checks the goalkeeper gloves, helmets and nets before the match. Why? | Broken gear can cause injuries | To make the match start later and last longer | Because gear sometimes changes colour | To count how much gear the club owns
test: hot day, been running, now dizzy n a headache | Rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Keep running, it'll pass if u keep going n don't stop | Go and lie in the sun | Drink a fizzy drink n carry on, the sugar fixes it
test: helmet's got a big crack but it still fits, use it? | No, don't use broken gear until it's replaced | Yes, if it still fits it still works | Yes, tape over the crack | Only for short rides round the block
test: mozzies always get me in the evening, why | Many mosquitoes bite most at dusk and dawn | They only bite at noon | They're asleep in the evening, so it must be something else | Mosquitoes only bite in winter
test: footy in the heat, drink only after the game yeah? | No, drink before and during too | Yes, water slows you down n gives u a stitch | Yes, drink at the end only, that's the rule | Only if you feel faint, otherwise u don't need it
explain: A boy in Arizona played baseball all afternoon in the heat and drank almost nothing; he collapsed with heatstroke and was taken to hospital.
explain: A girl in Florida did not use bug spray at dusk; she got many mosquito bites and later became ill with a fever the doctors linked to them.
fact: Heatstroke is an emergency: a child who is hot, confused or stops sweating needs to be cooled and get help at once.
fact: Mosquitoes can spread diseases such as West Nile virus and dengue in some places, so spray, long sleeves and nets matter.
test: hot as, didn't bring water to practice | Ask the coach and drink often, rest in the shade | Wait until you're home, it's only an hour | Drink at half time only | Fizzy drinks from the shop will do just as well as water
test: Why did the coach stop practice when a player felt dizzy and confused? | It can be heatstroke, which is an emergency | It was time for everyone to go and have their lunch | Dizzy players always play badly and lose | It was going to rain soon
test: there's mozzies everywhere, no spray left | Cover up and go inside or get spray | Don't worry, bites are harmless everywhere | Scratch the bites hard | Stay out longer so you get used to them
test: did u hear a kid at the footy carnival in the heat got all confused and stopped sweating and they rushed him off in an ambulance? real lesson? | Hot, confused and not sweating is heatstroke; cool him and get help | He just needed a nap in the sun | Not sweating means he's fine | Make him run it off
test: apparently half the team got head lice from sharing the same helmets. what's the true lesson? | Shared helmets are cleaned between users | Never wear a helmet at all, even on a bike | Lice only come from dirty kids | Helmets kill lice
test: my mate says u only get bitten by mozzies at midday so evenings are fine. right? | No, many mosquitoes bite most at dusk and dawn | Yes, they sleep all evening till the sun comes up | Yes, they only come out to bite in the rain | No, they only ever bite in the middle of the night
exam: Identify the signs of heatstroke requiring immediate assistance. | Being hot and confused, or ceasing to sweat | Being slightly thirsty after play | Feeling cool and alert | Being hungry before lunch
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response to signs of heat exhaustion? | Rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Continue playing until the end of the game, and rest thereafter | Run in order to cool down in the breeze | Stand in the sun to dry the sweat from the skin
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice before using sports equipment. | It is inspected for cracks, loose parts and broken straps | It is used without inspection if new | It is inspected only once a year, at the start of the season | It is used even if a strap is broken
exam: Ascertain the times of day at which many mosquitoes bite most. | At dusk and at dawn | Only at noon | Only during rain | Only at midnight
